<GuidePage
  title="Fat Quarter Size and Usable Cutting Area"
  guideSlug="fat-quarter-size"
  guideCategory="reference"
  description="Understand the common fat-quarter rectangle and why measuring the actual piece matters before counting quilt cuts."
>
  <section>
    <h2>A common fat quarter is about 18 × 21 inches</h2><p>
      It is typically half the fabric width by half a yard, but selvages,
      squaring, manufacturer width, and previous trimming can change the usable
      rectangle.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>The label is not the geometry</h2><p>
      Measure crosswise width and lengthwise length. Those edited physical
      dimensions must control whether pieces fit, not the preset name.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Test the actual rectangle</h2><p>
      Use the <a href="/calculators/pieces-from-fabric/"
        >Pieces from Fabric calculator</a
      > for one stock piece, then move to the <a href="/fabric-cutting-planner/"
        >project planner</a
      > for multiple remnants and purchase shortfall.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuidePage>
